Q: jQuery: Does not select correct dropdown value on page load A: <p>Use callback instead of calling the function: Please see below for the updated example.</p>

<pre><code>$(document).ready(function() {
    var hiddenFieldValue = $('#hiddenFieldId').val();
    //here no need to add the selected attribute, just set the value.
    $('#days').val(hiddenFieldValue);       

    $('#days').change(function() {
        //here just get the select box id to get value. This will get the selected option value automatically.
        var value = $('#days').val();   

        switch(value){
          case 'Mu':
            $('#mondayFields').show('slow');
            break;
          case 'Tu':
            $('#tuesdayFields').show('slow');
            break;
          default:
            break;
        }
    });     

}); 
</code></pre>

<p>JavaScript is async, so your previous is code does not guarantee that the function will be known during calling in your change method. </p>

<p>BTW: You can use $ instead of jQuery.</p>

Q: Will code in a Finally statement fire if I return a value in a Try block? A: <p>No. Example:</p>
<p>Run in Debug mode:</p>
<pre><code>        public static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            try
            {
                throw new Exception(&quot;Some exception.&quot;);
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                throw;
            }
            finally
            {
                Console.WriteLine(&quot;Processing in finally block.&quot;);
            }
        }
</code></pre>
<p>Console don't have text &quot;Processing in finally block.&quot;, only:</p>
<pre><code>Unhandled Exception: System.Exception: Some exception.
   at ...Program.Main(String[] args) in C:\Repos\...\Program.cs:line 28

C:\Repos\...\bin\Debug\SomeProgram.exe (process 1288) exited with code 0 (0x0).
To automatically close the console when debugging stops, enable Tools-&gt;Options-&gt;Debugging-&gt;Automatically close the console when debugging stops.
Press any key to close this window . . .
</code></pre>
<p>Let me repeat, while <strong>debugging</strong>. But sometimes we execute programs with attached debugger.</p>
